;;; One candidate, refuted in ONE firing — the cascade under `-L`, and its control
;;; under default flags. Regressions `forced-1-L`
;;; (`corpus/regressions.toml`).
;;;
;;; ── The program ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One object, and a rule that refutes any `(q ?x)`. Root is consistent; the
;;; blind rung's only candidate is `(q one)`, and it dies.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Root is saturated and consistent, and every remaining hypothesis makes it
;;; dead, so root is the one solution (Q-M1e.6, clause 3): **`Solution`, k = 1,
;;; a model with no `q` fact.**
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered before `fbcae3b2` ─────────────────────────────
;;;
;;;   default  Solution k = 1 — the lookahead killed `(q one)` before layer 1
;;;            and cached `(not (q one))`, so `alive` was empty and root was
;;;            recorded. Right.
;;;   -L       `No solution — the constraints are contradictory`, k = 0,
;;;            `exhausted = true`, **unsat core (0 facts)**. With the
;;;            lookahead off, `alive` was the singleton `{(q one)}` and the
;;;            forced-positive cascade wrote it into root as a premise-less
;;;            `<forced-positive>` fact; root was then contradictory and the
;;;            run was refuted. Promotion is valid only when root ∪ {h} is
;;;            consistent — here the right conclusion was `¬(q one)`.
;;;
;;; Now R is {∅}, root alone (`ein solve FILE`). The twin with the death two
;;; firings deep is `forced-2.ein`, which was wrong at default flags.
(relation thing T :why "{?3} is a thing")
(relation q T :why "q holds of {?3}")
(thing one)
(rule no-q () :match (q ?x) :assert (false) :why "q holds of {?x}, which cannot be")
(query :goal (thing ?x))
